Infrastructure Security: The Triple Shield Approach
Production-grade autonomous agents require a security framework that prevents them from modifying their own security boundaries or leaking sensitive data. The architecture adopts a "Triple Shield" approach integrating identity, content safety, and data protection.

Secret-less Identity and Intent Envelopes

Traditional API keys are a vulnerability. The architecture utilizes Microsoft Entra Agent ID for secret-less authentication, relying on managed tokens issued by the hosting platform. Furthermore, every action proposed by the cognitive layer is wrapped in an "Intent Envelope." This envelope is cryptographically signed and includes a nonce and timestamp to prevent replay attacks. A verification layer running before tool execution validates the signature and ensures the action adheres to the agent's defined capabilities. If an agent attempts to "Book a Flight" but suddenly calls a "Delete Account" tool, the Task Adherence API detects the drift and blocks the call.

Agent-to-Agent (A2A) Reliability via Dapr

For multi-agent systems, the architecture employs the Dapr runtime. Dapr provides mutual TLS (mTLS) for secure agent-to-agent communication and identity-based access control lists (ACLs). By running a Dapr sidecar alongside each agent instance, the system gains built-in service discovery, retries, and circuit breaking without requiring code changes in the agent logic. This ensures that the collaboration between specialized agents (e.g., a "Reader" agent and an "Explainer" agent) is secure, resilient, and observable.

