Recursive Health Monitoring

Traditional monitoring focuses on uptime. Level 6 monitoring focuses on "Reasoning Health." The MCP monitors the "Free Energy" levels of each agent; if an agent's prediction error exceeds a threshold, it suggests a model mismatch. This triggers an automated alert to the high-level orchestrator to initiate a "Safety Halt" or a "Model Re-calibration" sub-goal. This level of observability ensures that the system is not only running but is grounded in a reliable internal representation of reality.

Browser Integration: The Chrome Extension Architecture
To provide a seamless user interface and data capture mechanism, the blueprint incorporates a Chrome MV3 extension integrated with a FastAPI backend.

Agentic Web Memory and Data Flow

The Chrome extension acts as the "Perception" layer, indexing pages the user visits to build a semantic memory.

Capture: The extension extracts text and metadata, automatically skipping confidential domains via a denylist.

Indexing: Data is sent to the FastAPI backend, where it is chunked and embedded via Google text-embedding-004 or local Ollama models.

Storage: Embeddings are stored in a FAISS vector store, enabling hybrid temporal and semantic ranking—newer or more frequently visited pages surface more easily in search results.

Interaction: A WebSocket connection provides real-time, bidirectional messaging between the browser and the agent, allowing the agent to highlight snippets directly on the original webpage via native #:~:text= fragments.

This architecture turns the classic RAG pipeline into a "living memory system" where the agent remembers what the user reads online to provide deeply contextual assistance.

